## Target Register **(synthesize)**

> Guidance: whose speech, concretely — a region and the people the learner actually hears,
> never just a language name. One paragraph; the details of the variety live in
> `dialect.md`.

## The Weave Letter **(synthesize)**

> Guidance: what the learner's language carries and what the target carries, in teaching
> material. Derive it, don't assume it: where code-switching is native (many South Asian
> and urban varieties), loaned nouns in the learner's language are authentic and the target
> carries the load-bearing word; where it is not (most European pairs), switching happens at
> sentence boundaries and scaffolding stays out of the target sentence. Give one example
> sentence. Twin: `language.weave_rule`.

## The Modality Letter — read form and voice form **(synthesize)**

> Guidance: how the target is written on a surface the learner READS, and how it must be
> written for a voice to SPEAK it. For many languages they are the same (twin:
> `read_rewrite: false`). For a script the learner cannot read at speed, the read form is a
> romanization and the voice form is the script (`read_rewrite: true`). For writing systems
> with several layers (kana/kanji, hanzi/pinyin), name exactly which the learner reads.
> Twins: `language.chat_form`, `language.audio_form`, `script_regex`, `read_rewrite`.

## Telling Target From [Native language] **(synthesize)**

> Guidance: distinct script → detected by `script_regex`. Shared script → declared: audio
> writers wrap target spans in ⟦ ⟧ (the pack adds that rule to the audio prompts) and the
> renderer strips them. State which applies, and why.

## Word Boundaries and Inflection **(synthesize)**

> Guidance: spaces or not (`tokenization`); how inflection changes a word's end, as a
> regex or suffix list (`stem_tail`), and the narrower form a word takes inside a longer
> phrase (`host_tail`) — or "verbatim" when the language barely inflects.

## The Register Ladder **(synthesize)**

> Guidance: the rungs of address the learner must climb (child, peer, elder; or speech
> levels; or tu/vous). One rung is a valid answer. Name the rungs; the world's cast and the
> timeline's directions map onto them (`timeline.directions`).

## Lore Veins **(synthesize)**

> Guidance: 4–6 true story sources this language offers — etymology, words other languages
> took from it, its cousins and non-cousins, myth, film, cultural logic behind a register.
> Lore is curriculum (`constitution.md` → Stories Are Curriculum).
